const Modal = ({ children, isOpen, onClose }) => {
  const [showModal, setShowModal] = useState(isOpen);

  const handleModalClose = () => {
    setShowModal(false);
    onClose();
  };

  return (
    <div className="modal" style={{ display: showModal ? 'block' : 'none' }}>
      <div className="modal-container">
        <div className="modal-header">
          <h2>Modal Title</h2>
          <button className="close" onClick={handleModalClose}>
            &times;
          </button>
        </div>
        <div className="modal-body">{children}</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  );
};

export default Modal;
```
Let me explain what's happening:

* The `Modal` component takes three props: `children` (the content of the modal), `isOpen` (a boolean indicating whether the modal is open), and `onClose` (a function to call when the modal is closed).
* We use the `useState` hook to keep track of whether the modal is open or not. We initialize it with the value of `isOpen`.
* We define a `handleModalClose` function that sets the `showModal` state to `false` and calls the `onClose` function.
* We render a `<div>` element with a `.modal` class, which will contain the modal content. We use the `style` prop to set the `display` property to `block` if `showModal` is `true`, and `none` otherwise.
* We render a `<div>` element with a `.modal-container` class, which will contain the modal header and body.
* We render a `<div>` element with a `.modal-header` class, which will contain the title and a close button. The close button is implemented as a simple `<button>` element that calls the `handleModalClose` function when clicked.
* We render a `<div>` element with a `.modal-body` class, which will contain the modal content passed as a prop (`children`).

